WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:04.960
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute&nbsp;
Papers with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.960 --> 00:00:11.760
Look at this video of a moving escalator.&nbsp;
Nothing too crazy going on, only the escalator&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.760 --> 00:00:17.120
is moving. And I am wondering, would it be&nbsp;
possible to not record a video for this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.680 --> 00:00:24.160
just an image, and have one of these amazing&nbsp;
new learning-based algorithms animate it?

00:00:24.160 --> 00:00:30.080
Well, that is easier said than done. Look, this&nbsp;
is what was possible with a research work from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.080 --> 00:00:38.800
2 years ago, but the results are…well, what you&nbsp;
see here. So how about a method from one year ago?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.760 --> 00:00:46.640
This is the result. A great deal of improvement&nbsp;
- the water is not animated in this region,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.640 --> 00:00:53.120
and is generally all around the place, and we&nbsp;
still have a lot of artifacts around the fence.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.640 --> 00:00:58.400
And now, hold on to your papers, and&nbsp;
let’s see this new method…and…whoa!&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.240 --> 00:01:04.720
Look at that! What an improvement!

00:01:06.800 --> 00:01:09.840
Apparently, we can now give this one a still&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.840 --> 00:01:13.520
image, and for the things that&nbsp;
should move, it makes them move.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.160 --> 00:01:19.840
It is still not perfect by any means, but&nbsp;
this is so much progress in just two years.

00:01:20.720 --> 00:01:27.040
And there’s more! Get this, for the things&nbsp;
that shouldn’t move, it even imagines&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.040 --> 00:01:30.960
how they should move. It works&nbsp;
on this building really well.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.680 --> 00:01:40.800
But it also imagines how my tie would move around.&nbsp;
Or my beard, which is not mine, by the way,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.800 --> 00:01:48.640
but was made by a different AI, or the windows.&nbsp;
Thank you very much for the authors of the paper&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.640 --> 00:01:55.280
for generating these results only for us! And&nbsp;
this can lead to really cool artistic effects,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.280 --> 00:02:02.880
for instance, this moving brickwall, or&nbsp;
animating the stairs here. Loving it.

00:02:03.840 --> 00:02:08.880
So, how does this work exactly? Does&nbsp;
it know what regions to animate?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.600 --> 00:02:15.280
No it doesn’t, and it shouldn’t. We can&nbsp;
specify that ourselves by using a brush&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.280 --> 00:02:21.120
to highlight the region that we wish to see&nbsp;
animated, and we also have a little more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.120 --> 00:02:26.640
artistic control over the results by prescribing&nbsp;
a direction in which things should go.

00:02:27.440 --> 00:02:32.080
And it appears to work on a really wide&nbsp;
variety of images, which is only one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.080 --> 00:02:37.600
of its most appealing features. Here&nbsp;
are some of my favorite results,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.600 --> 00:02:43.840
I particularly love the one with the&nbsp;
apple rotation here. Very impressive.

00:02:44.880 --> 00:02:50.880
Now, let’s compare it to the previous method from&nbsp;
just one year ago, and let’s see what the numbers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.880 --> 00:02:56.800
say. Well, they say that the previous&nbsp;
one performs better on fluid elements&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.800 --> 00:03:02.960
than the new one. My experience is that it&nbsp;
indeed works better on specialized cases,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.960 --> 00:03:10.560
like this fire texture, but on many water&nbsp;
images, they perform roughly equivalently.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.440 --> 00:03:16.640
Both are doing quite well. So,&nbsp;
is the new one really better?

00:03:17.360 --> 00:03:22.560
Well, here comes the interesting part. When&nbsp;
presented with a diverse set of images,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.200 --> 00:03:30.080
look. There is no contest here - the previous&nbsp;
one creates no results, incorrect results,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.800 --> 00:03:36.240
or if it does something, the new technique&nbsp;
almost always comes out way better.

00:03:37.280 --> 00:03:41.920
Not only that, but let’s see what the&nbsp;
execution time looks like for the new method.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.560 --> 00:03:46.000
How much do we have to wait&nbsp;
for these results? The one from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.000 --> 00:03:51.200
last year took 20 seconds per image and&nbsp;
required a big honking graphics card,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.760 --> 00:04:01.040
while the new one only needs your smartphone,&nbsp;
and runs in… what? Just one second. Loving it.

00:04:01.920 --> 00:04:07.840
So, what images would you try with this&nbsp;
one? Let me know in the comments. Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.840 --> 00:04:12.640
in fact, you don’t need to just think about&nbsp;
what you would try, because you can try this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.640 --> 00:04:16.000
yourself. It has a mobile app, the link&nbsp;
is available in the video description,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.000 --> 00:04:19.680
make sure to let me know in the comments&nbsp;
below if you had some success with it!

00:04:20.560 --> 00:04:25.760
Here comes the even more interesting part.&nbsp;
The previous method was using a learning-based&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.760 --> 00:04:31.520
algorithm, while this one is a bona-fide,&nbsp;
almost completely handcrafted technique.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.320 --> 00:04:36.800
Partly because training neural networks&nbsp;
requires a great deal of training data,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.360 --> 00:04:43.920
and there are very few, if any training examples&nbsp;
for moving buildings and these other surreal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.920 --> 00:04:49.600
phenomena. Ingenious. Huge congratulations&nbsp;
to the authors for pulling this off!

00:04:50.640 --> 00:04:55.120
Now, of course, not even this technique is&nbsp;
perfect, there are still cases where it does&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.120 --> 00:05:01.200
not create appealing results, however,&nbsp;
since it only takes a second to compute,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.200 --> 00:05:08.800
we can easily retry with a different pixel mask&nbsp;
or direction and see if it does better. And just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.800 --> 00:05:14.720
imagine what we will be able to do two more&nbsp;
papers down the line. What a time to be alive!

00:05:54.480 --> 00:05:58.800
Thanks for watching and for your generous&nbsp;
support, and I'll see you next time!

